{{page-title "Animations"}}

{{!-- animations --}}
<div class="layout__section">
  <div class="layout__header">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <code>animation</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <div class="section__header">
        Intro
      </div>

      <div class="section__description">
        <p>
          CSS Animations provide more control over the animation process. You can define keyframes and control the
          animation duration, timing function, and delay.
        </p>

        <p>
          <code>animation</code> requires a <code>keyframes</code> definition to work. The keyframes define the
          animation sequence in CSS.
        </p>

        <div class="section__header">
          Keyframes
        </div>

        <p>
          The <code>@keyframes</code> CSS at-rule controls the intermediate steps in a CSS animation sequence by
          defining styles for
          keyframes (or waypoints) along the animation sequence. This gives more control over the intermediate steps of
          the animation sequence than transitions.
        </p>

        <div>
          <b>Keyframes example</b>

          <p>
            <code>
              @keyframes slidein {
              from {
              transform: translateX(0);
              }
              to {
              transform: translateX(100%);
              }
              }
            </code>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          On the <code>keyframes</code> example above, the animation will move the element from its original position
          to 100% of its width to the right. "from" and "to" are the starting and ending points of the animation.
        </p>

        <p>
          These starting and ending points can be defined with percentages, allowing for more complex animations.
        </p>

        <div>
          <b>Keyframes with percentages example:</b>

          <div>
            <code>
              @keyframes slidein {
              <br>
              0% {
              <br>
              transform: translateX(0);
              <br>
              }
              <br>
              50% {
              <br>
              transform: translateX(50%);
              <br>
              }
              <br>
              100% {
              <br>
              transform: translateX(100%);
              <br>
              }
              <br>
              }
            </code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section__body">
        <div class="demo-container">
          <div class="demo-item">
            <div class="demo-sun-animation-container {{if this.sunAnimationEnabled
                                                          'demo-sun-animation-container--animated'}}">
              <div class="demo-sun-animation {{if this.sunAnimationEnabled 'demo-sun-animation--animated'}}">
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section__controls">
        <div class="section__controls__title">
          Controls
        </div>

        <div class="section__controls__input">
          <button class="section__button" type="button" {{on 'click' this.toggleSunAnimation}}>
            Toggle Animation
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{{!-- end animations --}}

{{!-- animation properties --}}
<div class="layout__section">
  <div class="layout__header">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <code>animation</code> Properties
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <div class="section__header">
        Properties
      </div>

      <div class="section__description">
        <p>
          The <code>animation</code> property is a shorthand property for all animation properties (e.g.,
          animation-name,
          animation-duration, animation-timing-function, animation-delay, animation-iteration-count,
          animation-direction,
          animation-fill-mode, animation-play-state).
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Duration</b>: Specifies how long the animation should take.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Timing Function</b>: Specifies the speed curve of the animation (e.g., ease, linear, ease-in, ease-out).
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Delay</b>: Specifies a delay before the animation starts (optional).
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Iteration Count</b>: Specifies the number of times an animation should run.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Direction</b>: Specifies whether the animation should play in reverse on alternate cycles.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Fill Mode</b>: Specifies what values are applied by the animation outside the time it is executing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Play State</b>: Specifies whether the animation is running or paused.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Animation Name</b>: Specifies the name of the animation.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Animation Delay</b>: Specifies a delay for the start of the animation.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Animation Direction</b>: Specifies whether the animation should play in reverse on alternate cycles.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Animation Duration</b>: Specifies how long the animation should take to complete one cycle.
        </p>

        <p>
          <b>Animation Fill Mode</b>: Specifies a style for the element when the animation is not playing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <code>animation: 3s ease-in 1s infinite reverse both running slidein;</code>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{{!-- end animation properties --}}

{{!-- animation caveats --}}
<div class="layout__section">
  <div class="layout__header">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <code>animation</code> Limitations
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="layout__container">
      <div class="section__header">
        No control from JS
      </div>

      <div class="section__description">
        <p>
          CSS animations are not controlled by JavaScript. This means that you cannot pause, resume, or stop an
          animation
          using JavaScript. If you need to control the animation from JavaScript, you should use JavaScript animations
          instead.
        </p>

        <p>
          This means, that we have no control over stopping animations, reading its status or progress, or even changing
          the animation speed. CSS Animations are basic, but still really useful to give interactions interactive
          feedback, helping UX greatly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="section__header">
        No user input
      </div>

      <div class="section__description">
        <p>
          There is no way to interact with CSS animations. This means that you cannot pause, resume, or stop an
          animation using user input. The only workaround is to add or remove a CSS class to switch to a different
          animation or to stop the animation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="section__header">
        Animation logic lives on CSS code
      </div>

      <div class="section__description">
        <p>
          Debugging CSS animations is tricky. The animation logic is hosted on the CSS code, meaning that, for debugging
          purposes, you will need to inspect the element and check the CSS code to understand the animation logic and
          find the correct <code>@keyframes</code> value and modify it to try to reproduce issues.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{{!-- end animation caveats --}}